WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
In 2007, China sold the world
far more than it bought:

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.500
a current account surplus
of 9.8 per cent of its GDP.

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.400
The same year, the United
States ran a deficit

4
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.210
of 5.0 per cent; Spain,

5
00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.030
9.4 per cent.

6
00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:20.150
One country's surplus
is another's deficit.

7
00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.730
Some of these gaps lasted for years.

8
00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:25.140
So is a big gap between what a country

9
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.120
sells abroad and what it buys a problem,

10
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:30.030
and can it be fixed?

11
00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:34.930
In this mission, you'll weigh what
persistent deficits and surpluses can cost.

12
00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:37.380
Then you'll test two kinds of policy,

13
00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.440
and find out how far output
must fall to close a gap.

14
00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.450
Your mission: decide when a gap matters,

15
00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:46.370
and what a government can do about it.
